/* Let the wearable's own account send SMS through ModemManager.
 *
 * Why this file exists
 * --------------------
 *
 * Emergency SMS is sent with `mmcli --messaging-create-sms` (see
 * `src/indepensense/messaging/mmcli_sms.py`). ModemManager guards that
 * call behind the polkit action `org.freedesktop.ModemManager1.Messaging`,
 * which upstream defaults to `auth_admin_keep` — "ask an administrator for
 * a password, then remember it for a while".
 *
 * That default assumes a person is sitting at a desktop. The wearable has
 * no screen, no keyboard and nobody to prompt, and over SSH the session is
 * not even "active" as far as logind is concerned, so polkit has nowhere
 * to send the question and denies outright:
 *
 *     error: couldn't create new SMS:
 *       'GDBus.Error:org.freedesktop.ModemManager1.Error.Core.Unauthorized:
 *        Unauthorized: PolicyKit authorization failed:
 *        not authorized for 'org.freedesktop.ModemManager1.Messaging''
 *
 * Every emergency alert failed this way for a full test session while the
 * wearable said "Emergency alert sent to your guardian", because the HTTP
 * leg succeeded and nothing checked the other one. The spoken response is
 * honest now (see `telemetry/sms_alerts.py`), but honest-and-broken is not
 * the goal — this is the half that makes it work.
 *
 * Why this is not a security hole
 * -------------------------------
 *
 * polkit's model is "which *human* may ask the system to do this". There
 * is no human here: the sender is the device itself, acting on a button
 * its wearer pressed, and the message goes only to guardian numbers the
 * backend issued. The authorisation that matters happened when the unit
 * was provisioned with its credential.
 *
 * So the grant is deliberately narrow rather than blanket:
 *   - one unix user, not a group and not `unix-user:*`
 *   - the Messaging action only — not Control, not Location, not Firmware
 *   - `YES`, not `AUTH_ADMIN_KEEP`, because a cached admin password would
 *     expire and take emergency SMS with it at an unpredictable moment
 *
 * Running the whole runtime as root would also "fix" the symptom and is
 * strictly worse: it would hand a voice-controlled network service every
 * privilege on the device to solve one D-Bus call.
 *
 * Install
 * -------
 *
 *     sudo cp 50-indepensense-modemmanager.rules /etc/polkit-1/rules.d/
 *     sudo systemctl restart polkit
 *
 * No reboot needed; polkit re-reads rules.d on restart. Verify with:
 *
 *     mmcli -m 0 --messaging-create-sms="text='test',number='+639XXXXXXXXX'"
 *
 * A path like `/org/freedesktop/ModemManager1/SMS/1` means it worked.
 *
 * NOTE: `cknrf` must match `User=` in deploy/systemd/indepensense.service.
 * Changing the account means changing it in both files — there is no way
 * to share one value between a systemd unit and a polkit rule.
 */
polkit.addRule(function (action, subject) {
    if (action.id === "org.freedesktop.ModemManager1.Messaging" &&
        subject.user === "cknrf") {
        return polkit.Result.YES;
    }
});
